/* V76 — a tap that misses becomes a plan.

   ---- What is actually wrong, measured ----

   V56 measured a competent player at a median of ZERO hostiles on screen, with the arena empty 79%
   of the time and a hostile living 0.2 seconds. V57 re-derived the same number from Little's Law
   and put it plainly: a hostile lives 0.2 seconds because there is no reason on earth to leave it
   alive for 0.3.

   Both parts then treated that 79% as a hole to be filled. V56 added a spawn floor so there would
   always be something to tap. V57 paid 4.6x for killing low so the player would wait. One
   manufactures the crowding the owner complained about; the other pays the player to do nothing,
   and its own tuning note admits the multiplier had to be pushed from 2.6 to 4.6 before patience
   stopped being a trap.

   The 79% is not a hole. It is the half of the loop this game has never had.

   In Tetris that time is when you build the well. In Drop7 it is when you stack the board for the
   trigger. Here it is four seconds out of five spent waiting to be handed something to react to.

   ---- Why eleven versions of "make the tap smarter" missed ----

   Which member of the formation, where on its body, which one is sealed, which one must not be
   touched, how deep down the screen. Real depth, and every one of them is a qualifier on a
   reaction. The player answers questions the spawner asked, one at a time, and every question has
   its answer available within 200ms.

   V63's own header names what is missing, in a sentence about why cascades pay triangularly:
   "setting one up is the interesting thing the player is being asked to do." The game has never
   let the player set one up. V64 builds the formation, V65 marks the break point, V71 marks the
   trap, V67 announces all of it in advance; the player's whole contribution is recognition. The
   one exception is V72's positional splitter, and its header knows it is alone.

   ---- What this does ----

   A tap that finds nothing no longer finds nothing. It leaves a charge on the arena, and a charge
   is a **snare**: hostiles that drift into its ring are caught and held there, creeping instead of
   falling. They pile up. Nothing dies.

   Then you tap one of them, and every charge holding it goes off at once — killing everything all
   of them hold, and passing the blast on to your other charges.

   So the run has two moves instead of one. In the quiet you decide where the arena is going to
   collect; when it has collected enough, you decide when to cash it in. The longer you leave it,
   the more is in the ring and the more the ring is worth — and the closer the whole pile is to the
   reactor line you are trying to protect.

   ---- Why a snare, and not the two designs this part was built as first ----

   **Build one was a proximity mine.** Plant it, wait, the first hostile to touch it goes up. It is
   the obvious design and the measurements killed it:

     6s life, point trigger:   0 charges fired out of 9 planted in 75 seconds
     10s life, 56pt ring:      2 fired out of 15
     and a bigger blast:       1 fired out of 6 — but that one killed SIX hostiles

   and then a sweep of planting depth, which is the parameter a player actually controls:

     planted 22% of the way down:  5 fired of 8, 2.0 kills each, 1.03x the reactive score
     planted 38%:                  2 fired of 10, 3.5 kills each, 1.22x
     planted 54%:                  0 fired of 6
     planted 70%:                  0 fired of 12, 0.89x

   No depth gives both a firing rate and a payoff, and the reason is V57's own sentence: a hostile
   that walks into your trap is a hostile you could simply have tapped. A trap competes with the
   thumb for the same target, and the thumb is instant.

   **Build two made the charge a relay** — any blast that touched it set it off, so a tap could
   travel the screen through a path you had laid. It rides on the thumb instead of competing with
   it, which fixed the argument, and measured it fired four times in 55 seconds at its best depth
   and zero at three of the four. The reason is the number V56 recorded and neither build took
   seriously enough: **the arena is empty 74-82% of the time, with a median of ZERO hostiles on
   screen.** Any mechanic that needs two things to be near each other will almost never fire,
   because there is almost never a second thing.

   A snare does not need two things to be near each other. It *makes* them near each other. It is
   the only one of the three that attacks the emptiness directly instead of trying to build on top
   of it — density stops being something the spawner manufactures (V56's floor, which is exactly
   the crowding the owner complained about) and becomes something the player authored on purpose,
   in the place they chose, for a payout they chose when to take.

   That is also the shape the research points at from three directions at once: setup and payoff as
   separate moves the player times (Drop7), a growing pot that is at risk while it grows (Tony
   Hawk's balance bar), and a near miss you arranged yourself rather than one the game handed you,
   which is the version that actually makes people press again.

   ---- What it composes with ----

     - V21's heat becomes the charge economy instead of a punishment. Missing used to cost 17 heat
       and produce nothing; planting costs 12 and produces an object. The bar that used to say "you
       were sloppy" now says "this much of the arena is committed."
     - V63's cascade is the vehicle: a charge fires through `v63BlastHit`, the seam V63 left for
       exactly this — "anything the cascade can reach that is not in game.orbs".
     - V57's strike band pays its depth multiplier at the charge's own y, so laying the path low is
       worth more and is riskier.
     - V65's sealed hostile: a charge is a blast, so it strips both plates.
     - V71's mine is cleared safely and pays its bonus, as a reactor blast does.
     - V67's chevrons stop being decoration. They say where the formation is coming; that is the
       information you lay the path with.
     - V73's pilot still walks to your last tap, so laying a path and repositioning under fire are
       one gesture, and where you plant is a defensive decision as well as an offensive one. */

const V76={
 /* Inert for a moment after placing. Without it a fat-thumbed near-miss on the hostile you meant
    to tap plants a charge inside the blast you are about to cause and it is consumed in the same
    frame, which reads as the game eating your input. */
 arm:.32,
 /* Long enough to lay a path across several arrivals; short enough that the arena is never a
    museum of decisions made ten seconds ago. */
 life:9,
 /* Three. The player, three charges and one formation is already four things to hold in mind, and
    past four a human stops tracking objects and starts reading texture — which is the mechanism by
    which this game's density became noise in the first place. */
 max:3,
 /* Between V21's hit (7) and its miss (17). At the 34/s decay that sustains about 2.8 plants a
    second: fast enough to lay a path, far too slow to carpet the arena. */
 heat:12,
 /* The ring: what it holds, and what dies when it goes off. The drawing and the rule are the same
    number so they cannot drift apart — an earlier build drew the blast a victim would produce and
    triggered on a much smaller circle, which promised a reach it did not have. */
 reach:96,
 /* How far a charge reaches to DRAW hostiles in, as opposed to how far it holds them once caught.

    This is the part that makes the mechanic possible at all, and it took three failed builds to
    see why. A ring that only holds what happens to fall into it gathers almost nothing: the arena
    is empty most of the time and what there is arrives spread across the full width, so a 96-point
    ring in a 390-point arena catches one hostile and waits. A charge that BENDS the wave toward
    itself gathers regardless of where the spawner put things -- which is the difference between
    density the player hopes for and density the player caused.

    Horizontal only. Pulling on the descent as well would let a well park a wave indefinitely and
    turn the reactor line into a formality; sideways, a hostile still arrives on schedule, it
    simply arrives somewhere you chose. */
 pull:215,
 drag:150,
 /* What is left of a held hostile's descent. Not zero: a snare that stops a hostile dead is a wall,
    and a wall this cheap would end the game's only real threat. At 0.16 a caught hostile still
    arrives, about six times slower — long enough to be worth waiting on, short enough that leaving
    a full ring parked above the reactor line is a decision with a cost. */
 hold:.16,
 /* How many one charge holds. Above this the ring is full and further hostiles fall through it
    untouched, which is the pressure valve: a snare that held everything would be an auto-win, and
    the player has to be given a reason to cash in rather than to keep collecting. */
 cap:5,
 /* How far a blast may be from a charge and still set it off, as a multiple of that blast's own
    radius. At 1 the rule is exactly "if the explosion touches the charge, the charge fires", which
    is the only version a player can predict by looking. */
 link:1,
 /* Paid per hostile a charge destroyed, triangular for the same reason V63's cascade bonus is:
    catching a group has to be worth disproportionately more than catching one, or the placement
    was not a decision. */
 plant:240,
 /* Cyan is this game's "yours" — the pulse weapon, the reactor blast, the dodge spark. Red is
    theirs. A charge must never be mistaken for a V71 mine, the one object on the field with the
    opposite meaning, so the two may not share a hue. */
 core:'#5ef2ff',
 glow:'#e6feff'
};

/* The band a charge may occupy. Above V67's approach strip it would be behind the HUD; below the
   reactor line it would be under the pilot's feet. Clamped rather than refused: a refusal on a
   touchscreen reads as a dropped input, which is the lesson V21 paid for with its overheat
   lockout. */
function v76Band(y){
 return Math.max(V67.y()+18,Math.min(v73Line()-20,y));
}

const v76Begin=begin;
begin=function(mode='solo',seed){
 const out=v76Begin(mode,seed);
 game.v76=[];
 game.v76Planted=0;
 game.v76Fired=0;
 return out;
};

/* ---- Placing ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Whether the tap landed is read the way V21 reads it — from what the handlers actually did,
   rather than by repeating the hit test, which has moved twice already and would drift in a third
   copy. `game.v21Landed` is still set when V21's wrapper returns, and a defused projectile is the
   one case that leaves no other trace, so it is counted separately.

   Wrapped outside V21 deliberately. V21 has already billed a full miss by the time control comes
   back here, so the difference is vented rather than the heat table rewritten: 17 is still the
   right price for a tap that produced nothing, and this is a tap that produced something. */

const v76Tap=v12HandleTap;
v12HandleTap=function(clientX,clientY,event){
 if(!game.running||game.paused||!canvas)return v76Tap(clientX,clientY,event);
 const rect=canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
 const flying=game.projectiles.length;
 const out=v76Tap(clientX,clientY,event);
 if(!game.running||game.paused)return out;
 /* 'blocked' is a ward: the tap hit something, it just did not get through. Truthy, so it falls
    out here without a special case. */
 if(game.v21Landed||game.projectiles.length<flying)return out;
 if(game.v21Lock>0)return out;
 if(!(rect.width>0))return out;
 v76Place((clientX-rect.left)/rect.width*W,v76Band((clientY-rect.top)/rect.height*H));
 return out;
};

function v76Place(x,y){
 const list=game.v76||(game.v76=[]);
 /* The oldest goes, visibly, rather than the new one being silently refused. A cap the player
    cannot see spend itself is indistinguishable from a bug. */
 while(list.length>=V76.max)v76Fizzle(list.shift());
 /* Math.random, not rng(). `rng()` advances `game.rng`, the seeded stream the spawner draws from,
    so a cosmetic draw on a player-input path would make two players on the same daily seed meet
    different waves and would put a duel's two arenas out of step. */
 list.push({x,y,arm:V76.arm,life:V76.life,max:V76.life,held:0,spin:Math.random()*Math.PI*2});
 game.v76Planted=(game.v76Planted||0)+1;
 /* V21 billed this as a miss on the way in. It was not one. */
 if(typeof v21Vent==='function')v21Vent(Math.max(0,V21_HEAT.miss-V76.heat));
 ring(x,y,V76.core,30);
 audio.sfx('armor');
 haptic(9);
 if(!save.v76Taught){
  save.v76Taught=1;
  persist();
  toast('TAP EMPTY GROUND TO SNARE — THEN TAP WHAT IT CAUGHT');
 }
}

function v76Fizzle(c){
 ring(c.x,c.y,'#6f8ba8',16);
 for(let i=0;i<4;i++){
  const a=Math.random()*Math.PI*2;
  game.particles.push({x:c.x,y:c.y,vx:Math.cos(a)*26,vy:Math.sin(a)*26,
   life:.3,max:.3,r:1.5,color:'#6f8ba8',drag:.9,gravity:0});
 }
}

/* ---- Going off -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   A charge is removed from the array before it fires, not after. Its own blast re-enters `hitOrb`,
   which re-enters V63's cascade, which comes back through `v63BlastHit` — a charge still in the
   array during its own detonation finds itself and fires forever. Splicing first is the whole
   recursion guard, and the bound on the rest is V76.max: three charges can relay at most three
   times.

   Victims are struck on the rim rather than dead centre, copying V63's own link and for its
   reason: a hit at zero distance scores a PERFECT every time, and PERFECT is something a thumb
   earns. */

function v76Fire(c){
 const list=game.v76;
 if(!list)return;
 const at=list.indexOf(c);
 if(at<0)return;
 list.splice(at,1);
 game.v76Fired=(game.v76Fired||0)+1;

 ring(c.x,c.y,V76.core,V76.reach);
 ring(c.x,c.y,V76.glow,V76.reach*.45);
 explode(c.x,c.y,V76.core,22,.9);
 audio.sfx('blast');

 const before=new Set(game.orbs);
 /* Snapshotted before anything dies: a cascade splices and a splitter pushes, so iterating the
    live array while it is being rewritten skips hostiles. Everything the ring holds, plus anything
    else standing inside it — the ring is the promise, and a hostile visibly inside it that
    survived because the cap was already met would read as a bug. */
 const victims=game.orbs.filter((o)=>o.y>-o.r&&o.hp>0&&
  (o.v76Held===c||Math.hypot(o.x-c.x,o.y-c.y)<=V76.reach+o.r*.35));
 for(const o of victims){
  if(!game.orbs.includes(o))continue;
  hitOrb(o,o.x+o.r,o.y,true);
 }

 /* And on to the next charge. After the hostiles rather than before, so a relay path resolves
    outward from where the player struck instead of jumping to the far end first — which is what it
    looks like, and a chain that reads backwards reads as a glitch. */
 v76Relay(c.x,c.y,V76.reach);

 let k=0;
 for(const n of before)if(!game.orbs.includes(n))k++;
 if(k>0){
  /* On top of every kill's own score and V63's cascade bonus. This is payment for the placement
     specifically, which is the decision this part exists to create. */
  score(V76.plant*k*(k+1)/2,'chain');
  if(k>1)floating(c.x,c.y,`SNARE ×${k}`,V76.core);
 }
}

/* Any armed charge the given blast touches. Unarmed ones are skipped rather than queued: the arm
   delay exists so that the blast you are causing right now cannot consume the charge you planted
   in the same gesture. */
function v76Relay(x,y,R){
 const list=game.v76;
 if(!list||!list.length)return;
 for(const c of list.slice()){
  if(c.arm>0)continue;
  /* An empty well is not consumed. Measured, the firing sizes came back as
     [0,5,1,0,2,0,0,0,4,0,0,0,3,0,0,0,2,0,3,1] -- half of them caught nothing, because a well going
     off took its neighbours with it whether or not those neighbours had gathered anything yet.
     Spending a charge that has caught nothing is pure loss to the player and it is invisible: all
     they see is their own preparation disappearing for no reason. */
  if(!(c.held>0))continue;
  if(Math.hypot(c.x-x,c.y-y)>R*V76.link)continue;
  v76Fire(c);
 }
}

/* Deliberately NOT hooked to V63's `v63BlastHit` seam, though it fits there and an earlier build
   used it. With every blast in the game able to set a charge off, measured, ten of fifteen charges
   fired -- and killed 0.5 hostiles each, because they were being triggered by ordinary taps that
   happened to land nearby while the ring was still filling. A snare that anything can spring is a
   snare that is always empty when it springs. Charges now chain only to each other. */

/* ---- Holding ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The hold is applied by scaling whatever movement the base loop produced, rather than by writing
   to `o.speed`. Speed is not this part's to own: V21's divers accelerate on commit, the duel's
   gravity attack multiplies the whole field's fall rate, and V72 copies a parent's speed onto its
   halves. Multiplying and restoring a value three other systems write to is how two of them end up
   fighting over it and one silently wins. Scaling the delta composes with all of them and needs
   nothing restored — a hostile that leaves a ring simply stops being scaled. */

const v76Update=update;
update=function(dt){
 const list=game.v76;
 const running=game.running&&!game.paused&&list&&list.length;
 /* Positions before the base loop, for exactly the hostiles that are held. */
 const was=running?new Map():null;
 if(running)for(const o of game.orbs)if(o.v76Held)was.set(o,o.y);

 v76Update(dt);

 if(!list||!list.length||!game.running||game.paused)return;

 if(was)for(const [o,y0] of was){
  if(!game.orbs.includes(o))continue;
  o.y=y0+(o.y-y0)*V76.hold;
 }

 /* The pull. Strongest at the centre and falling to nothing at the rim, so a well has an edge a
    player can see rather than a cliff -- and so two wells side by side hand a hostile across
    instead of fighting over it in a jitter. */
 for(const o of game.orbs){
  if(o.y<=-o.r||o.hp<=0)continue;
  let bx=0,bw=0;
  for(const c of list){
   if(c.arm>0)continue;
   const d=Math.hypot(o.x-c.x,o.y-c.y);
   if(d>V76.pull||d<1)continue;
   const w=1-d/V76.pull;
   bx+=(c.x-o.x)/d*w;bw+=w;
  }
  if(bw>0)o.x=Math.max(o.r*.6,Math.min(W-o.r*.6,o.x+bx/bw*V76.drag*dt));
 }

 /* Re-read every frame rather than kept as a membership list: hostiles are spliced by cascades and
    pushed by splitters, and a stored list of who is held goes stale in exactly the frames that
    matter most. */
 for(const o of game.orbs)o.v76Held=null;
 for(let i=list.length-1;i>=0;i--){
  const c=list[i];
  if(c.arm>0){c.arm-=dt;c.held=0;continue;}
  c.life-=dt;
  if(c.life<=0){list.splice(i,1);v76Fizzle(c);continue;}
  let n=0;
  for(const o of game.orbs){
   if(o.y<=-o.r||o.hp<=0||o.v76Held)continue;
   if(Math.hypot(o.x-c.x,o.y-c.y)>V76.reach+o.r*.35)continue;
   if(n>=V76.cap)break;
   o.v76Held=c;
   n++;
  }
  c.held=n;
 }
};

/* ---- Cashing in ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Tapping a held hostile is not a kill, it is a trigger. Every charge holding it goes off, which
   takes the tapped hostile with it — so the player's thumb does the same thing it always did, in
   the same place, and means something new.

   Only a direct tap. A chain link arriving from someone else's blast must not detonate the ring it
   lands in, or a single cascade would empty the whole board and there would be no timing decision
   left to make. */

const v76HitOrb=hitOrb;
hitOrb=function(o,x,y,chain=false){
 const c=(!chain&&game.running&&!game.paused)?o.v76Held:null;
 if(!c)return v76HitOrb(o,x,y,chain);

 /* Reported as a landed tap BEFORE the blast, not after.

    V21 sets this flag inside its own `hitOrb` wrapper, which is further down the chain — so a
    cash-in that killed its target never reached the line that sets it, and two things went wrong
    at once, both of them punishing the exact action this part exists to reward. V21 billed the tap
    as a complete miss at 17 heat instead of 7, and the tap wrapper at the top of this file saw an
    unlanded tap and planted a SECOND charge on top of the one that had just gone off, spending a
    slot and more heat for it. The measurements that justified this part were taken with that bug
    present; it was scoring 1.75x against a reactive bot while being billed as a miss every time. */
 game.v21Landed=true;
 v76Fire(c);
 if(typeof v21Vent==='function')v21Vent(V21_HEAT.ventKill);

 if(!save.v76Cashed){
  save.v76Cashed=1;
  persist();
  toast('THE WHOLE RING WENT UP — HOLD MORE NEXT TIME');
 }

 /* It usually died in that blast. If it somehow did not — a sealed elite with plates left, an
    armoured hostile at full health — the tap still lands, as it would have. */
 if(game.orbs.includes(o))return v76HitOrb(o,x,y,chain);
};

/* ---- Being seen ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   Tapping empty ground has done nothing at all for this game's whole life, so the first time a
   player does it by accident the arena has to be unmistakable about what just happened — and for
   the rest of the run the charge has to be something they can plan around at a glance while
   looking somewhere else.

   The ring is drawn permanently rather than only on arrival. V63 makes the same call for the same
   reason — "a player cannot learn the reach of something they only see on the rare occasions it
   works, and learning the reach is the whole skill" — and here the ring is the plan itself.

   Everything rides over a dark backing stroke. The arena behind a charge is a lit planet, a nebula
   and a skyline, and thin bright line work on that without a backing is the exact fault V57's
   strike band and V74's impact markers each had to be rebuilt to fix. */

const v76Draw=draw;
draw=function(){
 v76Draw();
 const list=game.v76;
 if(!list||!list.length||!game.running||game.paused)return;

 const t=game.elapsed;
 ctx.save();
 ctx.lineJoin='round';
 ctx.lineCap='round';

 /* The path, drawn first so the charges sit on top of it. Two armed charges close enough to relay
    are joined by a line, and that line is the most important thing on screen once there is more
    than one charge: it is the difference between three markers and a route. */
 for(let i=0;i<list.length;i++){
  for(let j=i+1;j<list.length;j++){
   const a=list[i],b=list[j];
   if(a.arm>0||b.arm>0)continue;
   if(Math.hypot(a.x-b.x,a.y-b.y)>V76.reach*2*V76.link)continue;
   ctx.globalAlpha=.5*Math.min(1,a.life)*Math.min(1,b.life);
   ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(a.x,a.y);ctx.lineTo(b.x,b.y);
   ctx.strokeStyle='rgba(4,9,20,.8)';ctx.lineWidth=5;ctx.stroke();
   ctx.strokeStyle=V76.core;ctx.lineWidth=1.6;
   ctx.setLineDash([3,7]);
   ctx.lineDashOffset=-t*22;
   ctx.stroke();
   ctx.setLineDash([]);ctx.lineDashOffset=0;
  }
 }

 for(const c of list){
  const arming=c.arm>0;
  const k=arming?1-c.arm/V76.arm:1;
  /* Fades over its last second, so a charge about to expire is visibly about to expire and can be
     spent rather than lost. */
  const fade=arming?1:Math.min(1,c.life);

  ctx.globalAlpha=(arming?.55:.3)*fade;
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(c.x,c.y,V76.reach,-Math.PI/2,-Math.PI/2+(arming?Math.PI*2*k:Math.PI*2));
  ctx.strokeStyle='rgba(4,9,20,.8)';
  ctx.lineWidth=arming?4.5:3;
  ctx.stroke();
  ctx.strokeStyle=V76.core;
  ctx.lineWidth=arming?2.2:1.5;
  if(!arming)ctx.setLineDash([6,10]);
  ctx.stroke();
  ctx.setLineDash([]);

  /* How full it is. This is the number the whole timing decision is made on, so it is drawn as the
     ring itself filling rather than as a digit somewhere: the player is already looking at the ring
     and must never have to read a counter to know whether it is worth taking. */
  if(!arming&&c.held>0){
   ctx.globalAlpha=.16*fade;
   ctx.beginPath();
   ctx.moveTo(c.x,c.y);
   ctx.arc(c.x,c.y,V76.reach,-Math.PI/2,-Math.PI/2+Math.PI*2*Math.min(1,c.held/V76.cap));
   ctx.closePath();
   ctx.fillStyle=V76.core;
   ctx.fill();
   /* A tether to each catch. Without it a slowed hostile inside a ring looks like a hostile that
      happens to be slow, and the player never learns that the ring did it. */
   ctx.globalAlpha=.5*fade;
   ctx.strokeStyle=V76.core;
   ctx.lineWidth=1.4;
   ctx.setLineDash([2,5]);
   for(const o of game.orbs){
    if(o.v76Held!==c)continue;
    ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(c.x,c.y);ctx.lineTo(o.x,o.y);ctx.stroke();
   }
   ctx.setLineDash([]);
  }

  ctx.globalAlpha=fade;
  const r=arming?6+(1-k)*5:8;
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(c.x,c.y,r+3.5,0,Math.PI*2);
  ctx.fillStyle='rgba(4,9,20,.85)';ctx.fill();
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(c.x,c.y,r,0,Math.PI*2);
  ctx.strokeStyle=V76.core;ctx.lineWidth=2.2;ctx.stroke();
  ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(c.x,c.y,arming?2.2:3.4,0,Math.PI*2);
  ctx.fillStyle=arming?V76.core:V76.glow;ctx.fill();

  if(!arming){
   /* Three ticks turning slowly: the cheapest thing that reads as "live" without pulsing, and at
      three charges on screen a pulse would be a strobe. */
   ctx.strokeStyle=V76.core;
   ctx.lineWidth=2;
   for(let i=0;i<3;i++){
    const a=c.spin+t*1.1+i*Math.PI*2/3;
    ctx.beginPath();
    ctx.moveTo(c.x+Math.cos(a)*(r+5),c.y+Math.sin(a)*(r+5));
    ctx.lineTo(c.x+Math.cos(a)*(r+9),c.y+Math.sin(a)*(r+9));
    ctx.stroke();
   }
  }
 }
 ctx.restore();
 ctx.globalAlpha=1;
};
